<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kholodny Yar Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1919 CE – 1922 CE · capital Motronyn Monastery</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A partisan self-governing district in the forests of central Ukraine, which held out against Whites and Bolsheviks from 1919 to 1922.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kholodny-yar">Kholodny Yar</a> is a forested ravine area near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a>, rich in memories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> and the 18th-century haidamaka rebels. In 1919 villagers around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motronynskyi-monastery">Motronynskyi Monastery</a> formed a self-defence force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasyl-chuchupak">Vasyl Chuchupak</a>, which took control of some twenty villages and declared loyalty to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The district was governed by village councils and its partisan otamans, with the monastery as headquarters. The insurgents fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-denikin">Denikin</a>'s Whites and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, raiding railways and Soviet detachments, and kept contact with the exiled government of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ukrainian-peoples-republic">Ukrainian People's Republic</a>. Its forces numbered from a few hundred to several thousand fighters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasyl-chuchupak">Vasyl Chuchupak</a> was killed in 1920, and Soviet campaigns slowly broke the resistance. In 1922 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> lured several leading otamans to a fake meeting and arrested them, ending organised resistance. The story was later told in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-horlis-horsky">Yuri Horlis-Horsky's</a> memoir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kholodny-yar">Kholodny Yar</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kholodny-yar-republic.jpg" alt="Kholodny Yar Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: GlibThomas · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A5%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%B9%D0%AF%D1%80_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%80.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1919 CE – 1922 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Motronyn Monastery</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasyl Chuchupak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kholodnyi Yar Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=kholodny-yar-republic" aria-label="Kholodny Yar Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5425 463 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kholodny Yar Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5745 719L5697 681M5745 719L5697 681"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5745 719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5745 719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=kholodny-yar-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

